WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.739
Verification begins with a trust
relationship

2
00:00:02.739 --> 00:00:04.790
established outside the incoming token.

3
00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:08.521
A deployment may configure a public key
directly or use

4
00:00:08.521 --> 00:00:12.159
a trusted issuer's JSON Web Key Set,
called JWKS.

5
00:00:12.159 --> 00:00:15.237
Public verification keys do not need
secrecy, but

6
00:00:15.237 --> 00:00:18.781
their authenticity and association with
the issuer are essential.

7
00:00:18.781 --> 00:00:22.699
Private signing keys remain restricted to
the issuer's signing system.

8
00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:26.056
Configure the expected issuer and trusted
key source through

9
00:00:26.056 --> 00:00:29.601
application policy or trusted discovery
anchored to that issuer.

10
00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:34.637
A token cannot choose an arbitrary key
endpoint merely by including a URL.

11
00:00:34.637 --> 00:00:38.461
If multiple issuers are supported, select
only among preconfigured trust

12
00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:42.379
entries and subsequently validate the
token against the selected issuer.

13
00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:44.617
Never turn an unverified issuer string

14
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:46.669
into an unrestricted network destination.

15
00:00:46.669 --> 00:00:51.892
The key identifier, kid, is a lookup hint
within that trusted key set.

16
00:00:51.892 --> 00:00:54.411
Treat it as bounded, untrusted metadata.

17
00:00:54.411 --> 00:00:56.649
Do not concatenate it into filesystem

18
00:00:56.649 --> 00:00:59.727
paths, database expressions, shell
arguments, or URLs.

19
00:00:59.727 --> 00:01:02.898
Avoid unlimited cache entries indexed by
client-provided identifiers.

20
00:01:02.898 --> 00:01:05.883
Controlled library logic should select a
compatible candidate

21
00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:09.521
key; the subsequent signature and claim
checks determine acceptance.

22
00:01:09.521 --> 00:01:12.132
Cache key sets for reliability and
performance

23
00:01:12.132 --> 00:01:14.930
instead of fetching them on every request.

24
00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.822
Set refresh deadlines, network timeouts,
response limits,

25
00:01:17.822 --> 00:01:20.620
and request coalescing appropriate to the
issuer.

26
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.858
An unknown identifier may trigger a

27
00:01:22.858 --> 00:01:25.283
bounded refresh of the configured source.

28
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:28.548
If the key is still unavailable, reject
authentication.

29
00:01:28.548 --> 00:01:30.786
Repeated unknown identifiers must not
create

30
00:01:30.786 --> 00:01:33.211
unlimited outbound requests or expand
trust.

31
00:01:33.211 --> 00:01:35.823
Plan normal rotation by publishing new
verification

32
00:01:35.823 --> 00:01:38.621
material, changing the signing key, and
retaining

33
00:01:38.621 --> 00:01:41.419
previous keys for the intended
token-validity overlap.

34
00:01:41.419 --> 00:01:43.844
Retire old material according to policy.

35
00:01:43.844 --> 00:01:47.295
During compromise, retaining a compromised
key for normal overlap

36
00:01:47.295 --> 00:01:50.933
may be unacceptable; coordinate its
removal across verifier caches.

37
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:53.918
A key-service outage can permit use of
already

38
00:01:53.918 --> 00:01:56.716
trusted cached keys only within defined
policy.

39
00:01:56.716 --> 00:02:00.260
It never permits skipping verification or
accepting unrelated keys.

